memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to read and write jump buffers, handler tables, and the memory C callers hand the library"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to install signal handlers, send signals, run the shell, and end the process"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# D-164: signal installs a handler with rt_sigaction on Linux, as glibc's signal does (BSD semantics: SA_RESTART, the
# handler stays installed, the signal is blocked while its handler runs), returning through the runtime's restorer;
# raise is kill(getpid(), sig), so a handler runs before raise returns. Windows has no such signals: there the library
# keeps a table of handlers (glibc's signal numbers), and raise calls the signal's handler, ignores it for SIG_IGN, and
# for SIG_DFL ends the process with exit code 3, as the Microsoft C runtime's raise does; nothing outside raise and
# abort delivers a signal on Windows. abort raises SIGABRT, so a handler runs; if it returns, the default action is
# taken. machine.state bytes 27952 to 28479 belong to this module: from 27960 the Windows handler of each signal, 1 to 64.
handlersAt is constant
handlersAt purpose "Where the Windows handler of signal 0 would be, in machine.state; signal n's is 8n bytes on"
handlersAt type Int64
handlersAt value 27952

forever is constant
forever purpose "The bound of a loop that ends when its work does"
forever type Int64
forever value 9223372036854775807

invalidError is constant
invalidError purpose "EINVAL: an argument the function does not take"
invalidError type Int32
invalidError value 22

